---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Comments from '../../components/Comments.astro';
const P = '/misc/yosemite/';
---
<BaseLayout title="Yosemite, Four Years Apart — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Two visits to Yosemite: a hazy Labor Day weekend in 2022 with a dry Lower Yosemite Fall, the Mariposa Grove and Sequoia's Tunnel Log, and a June weekend in 2026 with the waterfalls in full flow and Half Dome from Glacier Point.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · September 2022 and June 2026</p>
  <h1>Yosemite, Four Years Apart</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">A hazy Labor Day weekend in 2022, a June weekend in 2026, and the same few viewpoints in between.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>Two visits · September 2–5, 2022 and June 6–7, 2026</strong><br />
    2022: Tunnel View → Southside Drive and the Merced River → Curry Village → Lower Yosemite Fall → Tunnel View again →
    Mariposa Grove → Oakhurst → the Tunnel Log in Sequoia National Park<br />
    2026: Tunnel View → Bridalveil Fall → Lower Yosemite Fall → Glacier Point → Fish Camp → Mariposa Grove → Turlock<br />
    One Tunnel View in haze and one in clear air · one waterfall almost dry, and the same one roaring ·
    one Grizzly Giant, twice · one ground squirrel
  </div>

  <p>
    This post is two visits to the same park. The first was the Labor Day weekend of 2022, with my wife: Yosemite
    Valley, the Mariposa Grove, and on the last day a detour south into Sequoia National Park. The second was my own
    trip, a weekend in early June 2026: the waterfalls, Glacier Point and the giant sequoias. Nearly four years and
    two very different seasons separate them.
  </p>
  <p>
    As with the other posts here, everything below is reconstructed from the camera roll: what was photographed,
    where, and at what minute. Neither visit was long, and neither camera roll is big, but a few spots turn up on both
    of them. Where that happens, I've put the two years side by side.
  </p>

  <h2>September 2022: Labor Day weekend</h2>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-2022.jpg'}><img src={P + 'map-2022.jpg'} alt="Map of the 2022 trip: Tunnel View, Southside Drive, Curry Village and Lower Yosemite Fall in Yosemite Valley, then south to the Mariposa Grove and Oakhurst, and on to the Tunnel Log in Sequoia National Park." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>The 2022 weekend, in the order the photos were taken.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    We drove up from Los Angeles on Friday, September 2, and got in close to midnight. None of the drive is in the
    camera roll.
  </p>

  <h3>Tunnel View in the haze</h3>
  <p>
    The first photo of Saturday was taken at 10:13 in the morning at <strong>Tunnel View</strong>, the overlook on
    the Wawona Road where it comes out of the tunnel and the whole valley opens up at once. Or it would have, on
    another morning. That morning the valley was full of haze. The granite walls on either side were pale shapes, the far
    end of the valley was gone completely, and only the pines in the foreground had any color in them. Next to the
    railing stands an interpretive sign titled "A Varied View", about how the view here changes.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-tunnel-view-smoke.jpg'} alt="Yosemite Valley from Tunnel View in thick haze: the granite walls appear as faint gray outlines and the far end of the valley is invisible, with dark pines in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Tunnel View, 10:13 am.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Southside Drive and the Merced River</h3>
  <p>
    From there we drove down into the valley and along <strong>Southside Drive</strong>. At 10:21, looking across a
    meadow, <strong>El Capitan</strong> was a pale wall in the haze, lit from the side, with a row of dark trees in front of
    it. A few minutes later we stopped by the <strong>Merced River</strong>. In early September it was low and slow:
    shallow, clear water running between boulders and gravel bars, with pines on both banks and the haze hanging in
    the trees behind.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-southside-el-capitan.jpg'} alt="El Capitan as a pale granite wall in hazy light, seen across a meadow with a row of dark pines in front." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>El Capitan from Southside Drive, 10:21 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-merced-river.jpg'} alt="The Merced River running low and clear between boulders and fallen logs, with pines on the far bank and haze in the trees." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Merced River, 10:26 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    A path runs along the river there, lined with a low curb of stones. At 10:46 we were at the east end of the
    valley near <strong>Curry Village</strong>, where the photo is of tall pines and the cliffs above them, still
    soft in the haze.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-merced-bend.jpg'} alt="A paved path with a curb of stones beside a rocky bend of the Merced River, with tall pines and a hazy cliff in the distance." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The path along the river, 10:29 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-curry-village-pines.jpg'} alt="Tall dark pines against a hazy granite cliff near Curry Village." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Near Curry Village, 10:46 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Lower Yosemite Fall, almost dry</h3>
  <p>
    At 12:38 we reached the vista point for <strong>Lower Yosemite Fall</strong>. In early September there was
    almost nothing falling. The photo shows a notch in the granite, a pile of boulders below it, and on the rock face
    only a few thin, dark streaks where water runs in other seasons. By then the haze had thinned and the sky above
    the notch was a clean blue.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + '2022-lower-fall-dry.jpg'} alt="A notch in gray granite cliffs above a boulder field, with only thin dark streaks of water on the rock where Lower Yosemite Fall should be." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lower Yosemite Fall, September 3, 2022, 12:38 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Tunnel View again</h3>
  <p>
    We went back to Tunnel View in the early afternoon, and at 1:13 the photo from almost exactly the same spot as
    the morning's looks like a different place. The haze was gone. <strong>El Capitan</strong> stood sharp and white
    on the left, <strong>Half Dome</strong> sat at the far end of the valley, the cliffs above Bridalveil Fall rose on the
    right, and there were white clouds over all of it. Three hours apart, from the same overlook.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-tunnel-view-clear.jpg'} alt="Yosemite Valley from Tunnel View in clear air: El Capitan on the left, Half Dome in the distance and the cliffs above Bridalveil Fall on the right, under white clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Tunnel View, 1:13 pm the same day.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The Mariposa Grove</h3>
  <p>
    Sunday morning was the <strong>Mariposa Grove</strong> of giant sequoias, near the park's south entrance. By
    11:43 we were on the boardwalk at the grove arrival area, with the red trunks of the sequoias rising on both sides
    and plenty of other people walking in with us.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-grove-boardwalk.jpg'} alt="A wide boardwalk with wooden railings leading into a grove of tall red-trunked sequoias, with visitors walking along it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The boardwalk at the grove arrival area, 11:43 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    A little further along the Big Trees Loop, a sequoia lay on its side next to the path, its trunk gray and
    furrowed. A few minutes later, beside an interpretive sign, another one lay with its whole root plate standing on
    end, a tangle of roots taller than the fence in front of it.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-fallen-sequoia.jpg'} alt="A huge fallen sequoia trunk lying beside a boardwalk railing, with tall trees behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A fallen giant beside the path.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-uprooted-sequoia.jpg'} alt="An uprooted fallen sequoia with its huge root plate standing on end, behind a wooden fence and an interpretive sign." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>An uprooted sequoia, 11:47 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Just after noon we were at the <strong>Grizzly Giant</strong>: a massive trunk
    that leans a little, with a fire-blackened hollow at
    its base and, high up, branches as thick as ordinary trees that bend out sideways. In the photos the people at its
    base are tiny.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + '2022-grizzly-giant.jpg'} alt="The Grizzly Giant sequoia: a massive leaning red trunk with a fire-blackened base and huge crooked limbs, under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Grizzly Giant, 12:05 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Lunch was at 2:09 at <strong>Smokehouse 41</strong> in Oakhurst, a barbecue place on Highway 41 below the park:
    a tray of ribs with coleslaw, mac and cheese, pickles and a roll, and a paper boat of sauced meat on the side.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + '2022-smokehouse-41.jpg'} alt="A metal tray of barbecue ribs with cups of coleslaw and mac and cheese, a roll and pickle slices, next to a paper boat of sauced meat." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Barbecue at Smokehouse 41, 2:09 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Sequoia National Park: the Tunnel Log</h3>
  <p>
    On Monday, Labor Day itself, we drove south to <strong>Sequoia National Park</strong>. The park has only one stop
    in my camera roll, at 2:33 in the afternoon on Crescent Meadow Road: the <strong>Tunnel Log</strong>, a fallen
    sequoia lying across the road with a passage cut through it, wide enough for a car. Up close it is mostly a wall of
    gray wood with a rectangle of green forest showing through the hole. After that, the camera roll goes quiet for the
    drive home.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-tunnel-log.jpg'} alt="The Tunnel Log in Sequoia National Park: a fallen sequoia lying across a paved road with an opening cut through it, green forest visible through the gap." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Tunnel Log, September 5, 2022, 2:35 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>June 2026: waterfalls, Glacier Point and the big trees</h2>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-2026.jpg'}><img src={P + 'map-2026.jpg'} alt="Map of the 2026 trip: Tunnel View, Bridalveil Fall and Lower Yosemite Fall in the valley, then Glacier Point, dinner in Fish Camp, the Mariposa Grove the next morning, and a late lunch in Turlock." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>The 2026 weekend, in the order the photos were taken.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Nearly four years later, on Saturday, June 6, 2026, I was back on the Wawona Road, and my first photo was taken
    at 1:03 in the afternoon at the same overlook as before.
  </p>

  <h3>Tunnel View in June</h3>
  <p>
    This time the air was clear from the start, the sky a deep blue with a few small, bright clouds. The view is the same
    one as in the afternoon of 2022: El Capitan on the left, Half Dome at the end of the valley, and the
    cliffs on the right. The 2022 photo cuts those cliffs off at the edge of the frame, so it can't settle the
    question for September. In June, though, there was a long white ribbon of water on them,
    <strong>Bridalveil Fall</strong>, visible even from the overlook.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-tunnel-view-clear.jpg'} alt="Tunnel View in September 2022: El Capitan, Half Dome and the cliffs above Bridalveil Fall under white clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>September 3, 2022, 1:13 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2026-tunnel-view.jpg'} alt="Tunnel View in June 2026: El Capitan, Half Dome and the cliffs on the right with Bridalveil Fall, under a deep blue sky with small clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>June 6, 2026, 1:05 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Zoomed in from the overlook, the fall drops from a notch high in the cliff and disappears into the trees.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + '2026-bridalveil-from-tunnel-view.jpg'} alt="Zoomed view from Tunnel View of Bridalveil Fall dropping as a white ribbon from a notch in the granite cliffs, with a tall granite face above it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Bridalveil Fall from Tunnel View, zoomed in, 1:03 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Bridalveil Fall</h3>
  <p>
    So that was the first stop. From the parking area at 1:25 the fall showed between the pines, a white stripe down
    a gray wall. A short trail leads from there toward its foot. At 1:34 the
    fall filled the frame, and the wind was blowing its spray sideways across the cliff and down over the boulder
    field below. People were scrambling around on the boulders in the mist, and I went up close enough to get
    soaked by it.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2026-bridalveil-pines.jpg'} alt="Bridalveil Fall seen between tall pines, a white stream of water dropping down a gray granite cliff under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Bridalveil Fall through the pines, 1:25 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2026-bridalveil-spray.jpg'} alt="Bridalveil Fall up close, pouring over a dark granite lip with spray blowing sideways across the cliff, above a boulder field." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Closer, in the spray, 1:34 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Yosemite Falls, in full flow</h3>
  <p>
    At 2:54 I was on the <strong>Lower Yosemite Fall Trail</strong>, the paved path that leads from the valley floor
    toward the falls. It runs straight between tall pines, and at the end of it, framed by the trees, both
    <strong>Upper and Lower Yosemite Fall</strong> were running, one above the other, white against the granite.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + '2026-yosemite-falls-path.jpg'} alt="A paved path between tall pines leading toward Upper and Lower Yosemite Fall, both running white down the granite cliff, with visitors walking on the path." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The trail toward Yosemite Falls, 2:54 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    At 3:09 I took a photo from the vista point for Lower Yosemite Fall, within a few yards of where the 2022 photo was taken.
    It is the same notch in the cliff, the same boulder field and the same pines on the right. In September 2022 the
    rock had a few dark streaks on it. In June 2026 there was a full waterfall pouring through the notch into a cloud
    of spray, and the viewing area below was packed with people.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-lower-fall-dry.jpg'} alt="Lower Yosemite Fall in September 2022, nearly dry: thin dark streaks on the granite above the boulders." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>September 3, 2022, 12:38 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2026-lower-fall.jpg'} alt="Lower Yosemite Fall in June 2026, a full white waterfall pouring through the notch in the cliff above a boulder field and a crowd of visitors." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>June 6, 2026, 3:09 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Glacier Point</h3>
  <p>
    Late in the afternoon I was up at <strong>Glacier Point</strong>, the overlook on the south rim, high above the
    valley floor. My first photo there is from 5:32. From up there <strong>Half Dome</strong> is
    straight across, its sheer face to the side, with the high country and its last patches of snow behind it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2026-glacier-point-half-dome.jpg'} alt="Half Dome from Glacier Point, seen across a valley, with distant snowy peaks behind, pines and shrubs in the foreground, and a thin white waterfall in the distance on the right." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Half Dome from Glacier Point, 5:33 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Below and to the right of it, two more waterfalls showed as white threads on the rock: <strong>Nevada Fall</strong>
    and, below it, <strong>Vernal Fall</strong>, the two steps of the Merced River on its way down into the valley.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2026-nevada-vernal-falls.jpg'} alt="Zoomed view from Glacier Point of Nevada Fall above and Vernal Fall below, white falls on granite surrounded by forest." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Nevada Fall (top) and Vernal Fall, 5:54 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2026-squirrel.jpg'} alt="A ground squirrel sitting upright on an orange-lichened rock among green shrubs." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A ground squirrel, 5:54 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Glacier Point also had a ground squirrel, sitting upright on a lichen-covered rock at the edge of the overlook.
    It held its pose long enough for a photo and a short video. At 6:11 the main viewing area was busy: people
    lined up at the railing and around
    the interpretive signs, with Half Dome and both falls behind them.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2026-glacier-point-overlook.jpg'} alt="Visitors at the Glacier Point overlook in front of Half Dome, with Nevada and Vernal Falls visible below it on the right and the high country beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Glacier Point overlook, 6:11 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The last photo from up there, at 6:23, looks the other way: straight down into <strong>Yosemite Valley</strong>,
    with the north wall of the valley opposite, its domes and cliffs in the low sun, and the green floor of the valley
    far below.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2026-valley-from-glacier-point.jpg'} alt="View from Glacier Point down into Yosemite Valley, with the granite north wall and domes opposite and the forested valley floor far below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Looking down into the valley, 6:23 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Dinner was at 8:24 at the <strong>Buffalo Bar</strong> in Fish Camp, just outside the south entrance: a
    bone-in pork chop with mashed potatoes, asparagus and sun-dried tomatoes.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + '2026-buffalo-bar.jpg'} alt="A bone-in pork chop in a cream sauce with mashed potatoes, asparagus and sun-dried tomatoes on a gray plate on a wooden table." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Dinner at the Buffalo Bar, 8:24 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The Mariposa Grove, again</h3>
  <p>
    Sunday morning was the Mariposa Grove, just as it had been in 2022. My first photo, at 10:14, was taken a few
    feet from the first photos of 2022, at the boardwalk by the grove arrival area. The sequoias were the same.
    The light was different: lower, earlier in the day, with the trunks partly in shade.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-grove-boardwalk.jpg'} alt="The boardwalk at the Mariposa Grove arrival area in September 2022, in bright midday sun." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>September 4, 2022, 11:43 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2026-grove-arrival.jpg'} alt="Tall sequoias and pines above the Mariposa Grove arrival area in June 2026, with the trunks partly in shade." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>June 7, 2026, 10:14 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Then the <strong>Grizzly Giant</strong> again, at 10:43. The two photos were taken from nearly the same angle and
    nearly four years apart, and the tree looks just the same: the lean, the hollow at the base, the great crooked limbs.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-grizzly-giant.jpg'} alt="The Grizzly Giant in September 2022, its leaning trunk and crooked limbs against a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>September 4, 2022, 12:05 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2026-grizzly-giant.jpg'} alt="The Grizzly Giant in June 2026, seen from below, its massive trunk and limbs against a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>June 7, 2026, 10:43 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    A little further on, at 10:54, was a tree I had not photographed in 2022: a living sequoia with a tunnel cut
    straight through the base of its trunk, wide enough for people to walk through. It is very likely the
    <strong>California Tunnel Tree</strong>. My last photo in the grove, at 11:10, is of the forest itself: tall,
    straight trunks in the sun along the trail, with nothing in particular to label.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2026-tunnel-tree.jpg'} alt="A tall living sequoia with a tunnel cut through the base of its trunk, standing beside a trail under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The tunnel tree, 10:54 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2026-sequoia-forest.jpg'} alt="Tall straight sequoia and pine trunks in sunlight along a trail in the Mariposa Grove." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>In the grove, 11:10 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    After the grove I left the mountains. The last photos of the weekend are from a late lunch at 2:26 at
    <strong>Texas Roadhouse</strong> in Turlock, in the Central Valley: a steak with green beans and fries.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + '2026-texas-roadhouse.jpg'} alt="A grilled steak with green beans on a white plate, next to a basket of fries, on a restaurant table." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lunch in Turlock, June 7, 2:26 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Two visits, side by side</h2>
  <p>
    Put the two camera rolls together and the difference is mostly water. On Labor Day weekend in 2022 the valley
    was hazy in the morning, the Merced ran low between the rocks, and Lower Yosemite Fall was a few dark streaks on
    a dry cliff. On the first weekend of June 2026 the air was clear, Bridalveil was throwing spray across its
    boulder field, Yosemite Falls was running top to bottom, and from Glacier Point you could see Nevada and Vernal
    Falls too. The trees did not change at all. The Grizzly Giant in 2026 looks exactly like the Grizzly Giant in
    2022, and the boardwalk at the grove still leads in under the same red trunks.
  </p>
  <p>
    The 2022 trip had things the 2026 one did not: the morning-and-afternoon pair at Tunnel View, and the Tunnel Log
    in Sequoia on the way home. The 2026 one had Bridalveil up close and the view from Glacier Point. Both started
    at the same overlook on the Wawona Road.
  </p>
  <p class="muted"><small>Maps built from the trips' photo timestamps and locations. Photo locations have been
